WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.320 --> 00:00:07.320
As of January 2018, anyone over the age of
21 can walk into a store in California

00:00:07.320 --> 00:00:09.160
and pick up some marijuana.

00:00:09.160 --> 00:00:12.040
All you need is some cash or a credit card.

00:00:12.780 --> 00:00:16.660
To learn more about how legalized recreational
cannabis sales work

00:00:16.660 --> 00:00:20.500
we tracked a product up the supply chain from
seed to sale.

00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:24.880
And we talked to cannabis entrepreneurs along
the way to find out what's changed for them

00:00:24.880 --> 00:00:26.940
operating in a legal state market.

00:00:26.940 --> 00:00:28.780
And what we found was surprising

00:00:28.780 --> 00:00:33.340
excitement, yes, but also anxiety about the
future of the legal market

00:00:33.340 --> 00:00:37.920
under California's highly regulated and highly
taxed system.

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:43.740
And industry insiders told us that if the
legal market remains so overtaxed and overregulated

00:00:43.740 --> 00:00:46.880
the black market will continue to stay in
business.

00:00:46.880 --> 00:00:49.660
The story ends with the sale of the Kiva bar,

00:00:49.660 --> 00:00:52.220
the product of a company run by a husband
and wife team

00:00:52.220 --> 00:00:55.080
who began working out of their Oakland kitchen.

00:00:55.080 --> 00:01:00.079
But let's start at the beginning about 100
miles away at Riverview Farms

00:01:00.080 --> 00:01:04.140
which two years ago converted from poinsettias
to pot.

00:01:04.140 --> 00:01:06.920
It's been a family owned business for 20 years

00:01:06.920 --> 00:01:10.520
with daughter Michelle Hackett currently serving
as president.

00:01:10.520 --> 00:01:14.240
This is the agricultural capitol of the world,

00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:16.700
fresh vegetables come out of this growing
region,

00:01:16.700 --> 00:01:19.460
we also have great pinot noir grapes coming
out of this region.

00:01:19.460 --> 00:01:22.880
There's no reason why cannabis shouldn't be
cultivated here.

00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:26.820
Hackett is excited for the future of the market,

00:01:26.820 --> 00:01:30.979
but under immense pressure from the increased
regulation and taxation

00:01:30.980 --> 00:01:33.160
that kicked in at the beginning of the year.

00:01:33.160 --> 00:01:37.680
Riverview holds 16 different cultivation licenses
to grow this amount of cannabis

00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:42.660
as well as 2 distribution licenses to transport
medical and recreational cannabis

00:01:42.660 --> 00:01:45.060
to retailers and manufacturers.

00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:48.020
They pay a harvest tax of $9.25

00:01:48.020 --> 00:01:51.740
per dry weight ounce of cannabis flower that
enters the commercial market.

00:01:52.440 --> 00:01:56.920
Hackett says that compliance costs have driven
up prices across the state.

00:01:56.920 --> 00:01:57.840
I think it's greed.

00:01:57.840 --> 00:02:01.659
People are looking for a handout and are thinking
that

00:02:01.660 --> 00:02:04.180
we're weed people, we must be making all this
money,

00:02:04.180 --> 00:02:06.119
but really we're running this like a farm,

00:02:06.119 --> 00:02:10.560
or another agricultural operation in the same
community wanting to be treated the same.

00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:13.819
The greenhouses here hold plants at all stages
of development

00:02:13.820 --> 00:02:18.060
culminating in mature plants ready for harvest
every 2 to 4 weeks.

00:02:18.060 --> 00:02:23.020
Then workers hang the cannabis to dry before
it's ready to be cut and sent to market.

00:02:23.020 --> 00:02:28.040
But Hackett says that while business was booming
in the era of medical cannabis legalization

00:02:28.050 --> 00:02:32.510
strict licensure requirements have shrunk
her customer base significantly

00:02:32.510 --> 00:02:35.820
since recreational legalization went into
effect.

00:02:35.820 --> 00:02:37.180
We have to protect our license

00:02:37.180 --> 00:02:43.240
so the only business we want to do is with
other licensed retailers, distributors, delivery services.

00:02:43.240 --> 00:02:47.040
In the past you had a customer list of, say,
maybe 300,

00:02:47.040 --> 00:02:49.080
right now maybe you're working with 50.

00:02:50.540 --> 00:02:53.500
It's the green boom and people are wanting
to jump on the bandwagon

00:02:53.510 --> 00:02:56.030
and see if they can profit from this industry

00:02:56.030 --> 00:02:59.420
but I think what people will realize is that
it takes a lot of work.

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:06.620
Our next stop is Kiva's manufacturing facility
in Oakland

00:03:06.620 --> 00:03:10.160
a chocolate factory with one extra ingredient.

00:03:10.160 --> 00:03:14.040
People want a product they can trust and rely
on

00:03:14.050 --> 00:03:18.709
and so in the sort of gray market of California
we were really able to stand out

00:03:18.709 --> 00:03:24.470
by producing something that was clean and
consistent every single time.

00:03:24.470 --> 00:03:28.940
Edible marijuana has provoked media stories
of hospitalizations and meltdowns

00:03:28.940 --> 00:03:31.440
with New York Times columnist Maureen Dowd

00:03:31.440 --> 00:03:34.820
once penning a column about the time she ate
cannabis infused chocolate

00:03:34.820 --> 00:03:39.220
and freaked out in a Colorado hotel room shortly
after legalization.

00:03:39.220 --> 00:03:42.560
But Kiva bars are hand packaged into distinctive
wrappers

00:03:42.570 --> 00:03:47.580
that foreground the psychoactive THC content
in big bold numbers

00:03:47.580 --> 00:03:52.239
so that users know exactly how much they're
consuming with each bite.

00:03:52.240 --> 00:03:56.020
The packaging and labelling on Kiva products
is extremely important.

00:03:56.020 --> 00:03:58.860
We treat every package essentially like a
billboard.

00:03:58.860 --> 00:04:04.560
The education process around cannabis and
consumers learning dosing is gonna take a while

00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:07.939
until people have the same familiarity with
cannabis dosing

00:04:07.940 --> 00:04:10.120
as they do with alcohol for example.

00:04:11.489 --> 00:04:14.360
Knoblich Palmer says that although the customer
base for marijuana

00:04:14.360 --> 00:04:17.860
has increased tenfold since recreational legalization

00:04:17.860 --> 00:04:23.300
that the increased taxes and regulation have
forced the company to make layoffs in recent months.

00:04:23.300 --> 00:04:26.280
The situation in the market right now is pretty
dire.

00:04:26.280 --> 00:04:34.720
Right now there's a few hundred licenses in
2017 there were maybe 20 to 30 thousand operators

00:04:34.720 --> 00:04:39.780
so we've seen a significant drop in the number
of people allowed to operate in the market.

00:04:39.780 --> 00:04:42.360
Taxes are a big component of the new market

00:04:42.360 --> 00:04:46.040
there are essentially four times that the
cannabis is taxed.

00:04:46.040 --> 00:04:50.460
It's surprising that with the legal market
in California

00:04:50.470 --> 00:04:56.729
that companies are experiencing less volume
and less demand

00:04:56.729 --> 00:05:01.140
and that has everything to do with the cost
of cannabis,

00:05:01.140 --> 00:05:03.680
the price of cannabis to the end consumer.

00:05:03.680 --> 00:05:08.580
Our company has unfortunately had to do a
layoff for the first time in our company's history.

00:05:08.580 --> 00:05:14.380
We were all really excited and kind of promised
a really healthy, robust cannabis marketplace.

00:05:14.380 --> 00:05:17.380
We're all sort of hanging on for our lives.

00:05:18.480 --> 00:05:22.380
Noblich Palmer says she's still optimistic
about the future of the industry,

00:05:22.390 --> 00:05:24.690
but that some major reforms still need to
happen

00:05:24.690 --> 00:05:28.020
in order for a legal market to outcompete
the black market.

00:05:28.020 --> 00:05:31.100
The end consumer is walking into a regulated
store

00:05:31.110 --> 00:05:33.410
and seeing prices up here

00:05:33.410 --> 00:05:36.880
and then they're walking into an unregulated
store,

00:05:36.880 --> 00:05:40.700
or they're calling their guy and they're seeing
prices down here.

00:05:40.700 --> 00:05:46.300
So the unregulated market really has the potential
to derail the entire system

00:05:46.300 --> 00:05:50.900
because it's undercutting all the legal operators
at every opportunity.

00:05:52.220 --> 00:05:56.140
Our next stop is at PharmLabs, a testing facility
in Long Beach.

00:05:56.140 --> 00:05:58.860
Before legal weed makes it to market in California

00:05:58.860 --> 00:06:02.500
the new law requires testing for purity and
potency.

00:06:02.500 --> 00:06:07.060
Often several times if the cannabis is part
of a manufactured product.

00:06:07.060 --> 00:06:11.780
It's one of many cannabis labs that existed
long before the state required testing

00:06:11.780 --> 00:06:14.120
since many companies, such as Kiva,

00:06:14.120 --> 00:06:19.400
underwent voluntary testing as a way to set
their products apart from the competition.

00:06:19.400 --> 00:06:24.090
The states don't have enough resources to
provide testing services.

00:06:24.090 --> 00:06:28.100
I think we're really filling in a gap, these
labs that have been here from the beginning.

00:06:28.100 --> 00:06:31.360
Dr. Robert Brodnick is the research director
at this lab

00:06:31.360 --> 00:06:34.510
and has a background in counternarcotics at
the Department of Defense.

00:06:34.510 --> 00:06:36.920
He says his mind changed when he saw that
cannabis

00:06:36.920 --> 00:06:40.120
could be a less harmful alternative to other
drugs.

00:06:40.120 --> 00:06:47.060
Cannabis is a potential way to mitigate or
offset some of the dangers of opioid abuse.

00:06:47.060 --> 00:06:50.771
This view is in stark contrast to that of
Attorney General Jeff Sessions

00:06:50.771 --> 00:06:55.160
who buys into the marijuana as gateway drug
argument.

00:06:55.160 --> 00:06:57.289
Heroin addiction starts with prescriptions.

00:06:57.289 --> 00:07:01.680
We think a lot of this is starting with marijuana
and other drugs too.

00:07:01.680 --> 00:07:03.100
Studies are still limited,

00:07:03.100 --> 00:07:06.860
but preliminary data do show that states with
legal marijuana

00:07:06.860 --> 00:07:10.960
tend to have lower rates of opioid addiction
and overdose deaths.

00:07:10.960 --> 00:07:14.880
And while testing should make cannabis products
even safer for consumers,

00:07:14.880 --> 00:07:19.600
Magdoff worries that California's testing
standards are a little too stringent.

00:07:19.610 --> 00:07:22.050
I think it's gonna be a rough start for California

00:07:22.050 --> 00:07:24.620
with the amount of products that are gonna
fail.

00:07:24.620 --> 00:07:27.240
The pesticide thresholds are very stringent
here.

00:07:27.240 --> 00:07:30.980
Cannabis is the most tested agricultural commodity
at this point.

00:07:30.980 --> 00:07:33.960
For California cannabis industry to be successful

00:07:33.960 --> 00:07:36.640
there needs to be kind of a middle ground.

00:07:38.380 --> 00:07:41.420
The final destination is, of course, the storefront.

00:07:41.420 --> 00:07:44.660
Known for years and years in California as
the dispensary.

00:07:44.660 --> 00:07:47.310
We visited A Green Alternative in San Diego

00:07:47.310 --> 00:07:49.949
just minutes from the border and, ironically,

00:07:49.949 --> 00:07:53.340
down the road from the Drug Enforcement Administration offices.

00:07:53.340 --> 00:07:56.160
The store counts Kiva as one of its top sellers

00:07:56.160 --> 00:08:00.480
and owner Zachary Lazarus says he's seen a
definite spike in sales

00:08:00.480 --> 00:08:02.440
post recreational legalization.

00:08:02.440 --> 00:08:05.580
The difference between operating medicinally
versus recreationally

00:08:05.590 --> 00:08:09.850
is you have a plethora of people that have
come out of the woodwork,

00:08:09.850 --> 00:08:16.440
have come out of the closet, that are so happy
to be able to consume cannabis for the first time

00:08:16.440 --> 00:08:20.300
or to re look at it since their college or
high school days.

00:08:20.310 --> 00:08:22.830
But, like the other business owners we spoke
with,

00:08:22.830 --> 00:08:26.220
he says that regulations are still too tight,
taxes too high,

00:08:26.220 --> 00:08:28.760
and worries that local governments are too
involved

00:08:28.760 --> 00:08:32.500
in picking winners and losers in the market
with the licensing system.

00:08:32.500 --> 00:08:35.680
It seems like there's a lot of barriers to
entry right now

00:08:35.690 --> 00:08:37.620
but that doesn't have to be that way.

00:08:37.620 --> 00:08:40.380
Yes we need strict compliance and we need
accountability

00:08:40.380 --> 00:08:45.500
but we also need to give all those mom and
pop shop owners an opportunity

00:08:45.500 --> 00:08:49.500
a chance to give back with taxation, a chance
to grow jobs

00:08:49.500 --> 00:08:53.320
and a chance to really contribute to the cannabis
community.

00:08:53.320 --> 00:08:55.780
And like everyone in the cannabis industry,

00:08:55.780 --> 00:08:59.920
Lazarus still lives under the dark cloud of
federal prohibition.

00:08:59.920 --> 00:09:04.240
Stores like his still have trouble even opening
bank accounts.

00:09:04.240 --> 00:09:08.000
We don't have a fair shake when it comes to
being able to transact

00:09:08.000 --> 00:09:10.860
and do it above board like everybody else.

00:09:10.860 --> 00:09:14.100
Issues with the banking system and fear of
federal crackdowns

00:09:14.100 --> 00:09:17.150
were a consistent theme every step of the
way.

00:09:17.150 --> 00:09:21.960
It's scary, I'm not gonna lie, it's a huge
issue for us in terms of banking.

00:09:21.960 --> 00:09:26.500
Having us federally legal would take a huge,
you know, weight off my shoulder.

00:09:26.500 --> 00:09:29.180
Cannabis being illegal at the federal level

00:09:29.180 --> 00:09:36.000
is certainly a challenge that the industry
thinks about on a daily basis.

00:09:36.000 --> 00:09:40.520
It's a natural governor of the growth potential
of the industry.

00:09:40.520 --> 00:09:44.280
The words coming out of Jeff Sessions' mouth,
to me,

00:09:44.280 --> 00:09:49.780
is like the words coming out of Anslinger's
mouth back in the 1930s.

00:09:49.780 --> 00:09:54.200
You have a lot of criticism and you have a
lot of ignorance

00:09:54.200 --> 00:09:55.900
that really doesn't understand an industry.

00:09:55.900 --> 00:09:59.320
When you don't understand something you tend
to criticize it.

00:09:59.320 --> 00:10:03.800
But despite lingering uncertainty, everyone
along the supply chain remained optimistic

00:10:03.800 --> 00:10:07.420
that there will be regulatory reforms in California

00:10:07.420 --> 00:10:10.420
and eventual legalization nationwide.

00:10:10.420 --> 00:10:12.020
The cat's out of the bag.

00:10:12.020 --> 00:10:17.240
More people in this country live in a state
with some type of cannabis regulation now

00:10:17.240 --> 00:10:19.160
so I really feel there's no going back.

00:10:19.160 --> 00:10:23.600
It's not going away, so they might as well
regulate it and capitalize on the tax money.

00:10:23.600 --> 00:10:25.820
The whole state really wants this

00:10:25.820 --> 00:10:29.580
with 37 and a half million people in our state
you really can't go backwards

00:10:29.580 --> 00:10:32.300
I mean, that's just reality.

00:10:32.300 --> 00:10:38.740
California's going to recreational market
is a huge win for the industry

00:10:38.740 --> 00:10:40.640
for the cannabis plant overall.

00:10:40.640 --> 00:10:46.540
It will really help cannabis become more mainstream,
allow more people to have access to it

00:10:46.540 --> 00:10:50.220
and really further the movement in a way that
we probably have never seen before.

